From: Arch POWER
Subject: [PATCH] OpenEXRCore: qualify isnan/isinf for C++ translation units

internal_coding.h is a C header, but it is included from C++ sources in
src/test/OpenEXRCoreTest. float_to_uint() and its neighbours call isnan()
and isinf() unqualified, relying on the <math.h> macros.

In C++ libstdc++ undefines those macros and declares the functions in
namespace std. GCC 16 no longer also injects them into the global
namespace, so every C++ consumer fails:

  internal_coding.h:257:21: error: isnan was not declared in this scope;
                                   did you mean std::isnan?

Observed building openexr 3.4.13 with GCC 16.1.1.

---
--- a/src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_coding.h
+++ b/src/lib/OpenEXRCore/internal_coding.h
@@ -31,6 +31,11 @@
 #endif
 
 #include <math.h>
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+#    include <cmath>
+using std::isinf;
+using std::isnan;
+#endif
 
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 extern "C" {
